data.content:
`SafeAreaEdges="None"` only tells MAUI not to add padding for safe areas—it doesn't make the OS draw your content behind the system bars. You likely need two additional things:

**1. Hide or extend behind the Shell/NavigationPage chrome:**

If your page is in a `Shell` or `NavigationPage`, hide the nav bar:

```xml
<ContentPage ...
    Shell.NavBarIsVisible="False"
    Shell.TabBarIsVisible="False"
    SafeAreaEdges="None">
```

**2. Enable edge-to-edge rendering (Android):**

Android doesn't draw behind system bars by default. In your `Platforms/Android/MainActivity.cs` (or a page-specific handler), opt in:

```csharp
#if ANDROID
using AndroidX.Core.View;

// In your MainActivity or page lifecycle:
WindowCompat.SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(Window, false);
Window.SetStatusBarColor(Android.Graphics.Color.Transparent);
Window.SetNavigationBarColor(Android.Graphics.Color.Transparent);
#endif
```

Or in .NET 10, you can use the MAUI lifecycle event:

```csharp
// In MauiProgram.cs or App.xaml.cs
Microsoft.Maui.Handlers.WindowHandler.Mapper.AppendToMapping("EdgeToEdge", (handler, view) =>
{
#if ANDROID
    var window = handler.PlatformView;
    WindowCompat.SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(window, false);
    window.SetStatusBarColor(Android.Graphics.Color.Transparent);
    window.SetNavigationBarColor(Android.Graphics.Color.Transparent);
#endif
});
```

**3. iOS** — `SafeAreaEdges="None"` should be sufficient on iOS as long as the nav bar is hidden.

**TL;DR:** `SafeAreaEdges="None"` prevents MAUI from adding inset padding, but Android still clips content to below the status bar unless you call `SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(window, false)` and make the bars transparent.
